  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will remain in control through this week and into the \nstart of next week. A weak cold front will move through today, which \ncould keep temperatures a couple degrees cooler than yesterday. \nWinds will be breezy as the front moves through. A Nor'easter along \nthe coast will inch closer to the coastline Wednesday night into \nThursday. Precipitation from this system is not expected to extend \nmuch inland, though with a tightening pressure gradient, winds will \nbecome gusty. At the same time, a cold front approaches from the \nnorthwest. Most models have it running out of moisture by the time \nit enters the region, but the ECMWF has been consistent over the \nlast several runs now with showing minimal amounts of precipitation \nfor parts of the region. With 1000-500mb RH values at around 60% in \nthe ECMWF, moisture is even limited with that solution. If anything \ncan develop during this time, it will likely be very light. A \nslight cool down is expected at the end of the week following \nthe frontal passage, though temperatures will rebound over the \nweekend.\n\nAs needed over the last few forecast updates, adjustments were made \nto temperatures and dewpoints. Sunny skies have led to temperatures \nclimbing above what most guidance had. Meanwhile, these clear skies \nhave then led to temperatures tanking at night while an inversion \nhas kept the higher elevations more mild. During peak heating, \ndewpoints have been significantly lowered as model guidance have\noften been too high.",
